DEVIATIONS from the slice prompt's strict allowed-files list:

  D1. The AlertStudioPage actually lives at
      web/src/features/notifications/pages/AlertStudioPage.tsx, NOT
      under web/src/features/alerts/. The /alerts/* SPA paths
      (legacy) redirect to /notifications/* (canonical) — verified
      by reading web/src/App.tsx. The slice prompt's allowed-files
      list says "web/src/features/alerts/**" but the only AlertStudio
      route in the SPA mounts the notifications/ page. Mounting
      AINLAlertBuilder anywhere else would yield a hidden surface
      that the off-mode contract test cannot exercise.
      → Mounted in web/src/features/notifications/pages/AlertStudioPage.tsx
        and tested under web/src/features/notifications/__tests__/.
      → The registry's Routes.Frontend still lists "/alerts/studio"
        verbatim per the prompt's spec (the registry is the contract
        published to the SPA; the actual mount is an implementation
        detail).
      → Same pattern as slice 0014's diagnostics-vs-analytics
        deviation; precedent already in the log lineage.

  D2. web/src/ai/features.ts is regenerated by `go run ./tools/aigen`
      (not in the strict allowed list) but is the canonical mirror
      of internal/ai/features/registry.go — a registry change that
      did NOT regenerate the TS mirror would be caught by
      `go run ./tools/aigen --check` and the aivet final-gate.
      → The aigen tool is the documented method to update this file
        and was used here exactly as in slices 0012 / 0013 / 0014.

  D3. internal/ai/tools/validate.go received a generic `omitempty`
      keyword fix in applyRulesRuntime so an optional-field with a
      `oneof` constraint (used here by alertRuleDraftInput.TriggerMode)
      can be legitimately omitted by the LLM. Without this fix the
      validator would reject every draft that legitimately omits
      trigger_mode (defaulting to "repeat" inside the tool).
      → omitempty is a standard go-playground/validator keyword the
        schema generator already silently ignored; the fix only
        adds the runtime side. Backward-compatible: existing rules
        without omitempty are unchanged.
      → Slice prompt allows internal/ai/tools/** "only for reusable
        typed tools required by this slice" — this fix is structurally
        required for the alert-builder tools and is reusable for any
        future slice with optional oneof fields.

=== REASONING ===

Why the design preserves ADR-015 §I1, §I3, §I4, §I5, §I6, §I7,
§I9, §I10, §I12 and the non-AI baseline:

I1 default-off:    The new feature `nl-alert-builder` lands in the
   registry with DefaultOn=false. NeedsTools=true and
   NeedsStream=true so the per-feature gate also requires the user
   to first set ai_mode to local/cloud and then explicitly toggle
   nl-alert-builder on. The Settings UI walks features.Registry
   alphabetically; the nl-alert-builder toggle ships off.

I3 baseline intact: AlertHandler.CreateRule + validateAlertRule +
   the AlertStudioPage manual form are completely untouched by
   this slice. The AI surface is a NEW conditionally-rendered
   <FadeIn><AINLAlertBuilder /></FadeIn> block injected at the
   top of the existing page; when the gate is off, ZERO additional
   markup is rendered. The save button and form schema are
   identical to before.
   → Proven by TestNLAlertBuilderAIOffHidesPanelAndManualFormWorks
     (Go side) which probes the typed POST /api/v1/alerts/rules
     handler → 201 + "origin":"manual" + "ai":false markers.
   → Proven by the same-name Vitest test which renders the
     wrapped AINLAlertBuilder with mode='off' and asserts the
     container is empty (no AI markup).

I4 zero egress:    No new outbound HTTP, MQTT, or filesystem path
   leaves the API container. The strategy uses the same provider
   registry (default mode='off' = mock provider with canned
   replies). Every provider call still flows through the locked
   decorator chain: redaction → rate limit → cost cap → audit →
   trace.

I5 hidden UI:      withAiFeature('nl-alert-builder', InnerSection)
   evaluates the gate at render time using useSettings(). When
   ai_mode='off' the HOC returns null — the wrapper div is NOT in
   the DOM at all, so screen.queryByTestId(...) returns null AND
   container is empty. Both invariants are asserted by the Vitest.

I6 404 routes:     POST /api/v1/ai/alerts/rules/draft is wrapped by
   guard.Wrap("nl-alert-builder", handler). The guard reads
   settings.ai_mode + settings.ai_features[id] and short-circuits
   to chi.NewRouter().NotFound (the standard "404 page not found")
   BEFORE the handler runs in off mode. The 404 body is the chi
   default — verified by the Go test asserting "nl-alert-builder",
   "feature", "strategy", "provider", "draft", "alert-builder"
   are all absent from the off-mode 404 body.

I7 per-feature:    The guard checks BOTH ai_mode AND
   settings.ai_features["nl-alert-builder"]. Either being false
   yields 404. Verified by 3 Vitest cases:
     - mode=off + toggle=true   → empty (mode trumps toggle)
     - mode=cloud + toggle=false → empty (toggle trumps mode)
     - mode=cloud + toggle=true  → renders (positive control)

I9 redaction:      RedactionPolicy() returns the deny-all
   PolicyAlertBuilder. Identifiers (vehicle IDs, signal names) flow
   through tool ARGUMENTS, not prompt TEXT — the system prompt
   instructs the LLM to never put identifiers in prose. The
   round-trip flag is false because there are no allowed classes
   to round-trip.

I10 type system:   The AI surface lives entirely under
   /api/v1/ai/*. No field on the canonical AlertRule JSON shape is
   added or modified by this slice. The AI tools (draft_alert_rule,
   validate_alert_rule) accept a TYPED alertRuleDraftInput / 
   alertRuleValidateInput → marshalled to a *models.AlertRule →
   delegated to the canonical validateAlertRule (same code path
   as the typed handler).

I12 client/bg:     This slice adds:
     - Service worker chunk:      ai-nl-alert-builder
     - Client storage key:        ai.alertBuilder.draft
     - No background jobs added.
     - No push kinds added.
   The chunk + storage key are declared in the registry's
   off-mode-contract metadata. When ai_mode='off' the wrapped
   component is absent so neither the chunk nor the storage key
   is touched on the client side.

PROPOSE-only contract (the slice prompt's load-bearing semantic
constraint):

  - draft_alert_rule.Mutates() = false
  - validate_alert_rule.Mutates() = false
  - The strategy's allowedTools are ONLY these two pure-functional
    tools. No save_alert_rule, no delete_alert_rule, no
    update_alert_rule.
  - The dispatcher's denyAllConfirm hook is wired anyway as
    defence-in-depth: a future strategy edit that accidentally
    adds a mutating tool would be REJECTED at runtime.
  - The actual save flows through the canonical typed POST
    /api/v1/alerts/rules handler ONLY when the user explicitly
    clicks Save in the AlertStudio form. Pinned in the
    TestNLAlertBuilderAIOffHidesPanelAndManualFormWorks Go test.
  - buildDraftRule unconditionally CLAMPS VehicleIDs to the
    caller-supplied vehicle, even if the LLM proposes a different
    ID — defence-in-depth on top of the system prompt's
    refuse-cross-vehicle instruction. Pinned in
    TestDraftAlertRule_ScopeClampOverridesLLMVehicleID.

